BUG-008 — CSV export missing UTF-8 BOM

Bug ID: BUG-008
Title: CSV export is missing the required UTF-8 Byte Order Mark (BOM)
Severity: Medium
Priority: P2
Area : Exporting and checking CSV file
Testing: Manual
Status: Open
Environment: Local application — http://localhost:4000
Module: Staff Booking / CSV Export
Type: Functional / Data Interoperability

Preconditions
User is authenticated as a Staff user.
At least one booking exists in the system.
Staff CSV export is accessible.
Steps to Reproduce
Log in to the application as a Staff user.
Navigate to the Staff bookings section.
Export the bookings using the CSV export option.
Open the downloaded CSV file in a hex/byte viewer or inspect the response body.
Check the first three bytes of the file.
Test Data

Expected UTF-8 BOM:

EF BB BF
Expected Result

The CSV file should begin with the UTF-8 BOM:

EF BB BF

This is required by SPEC.md §6.5 so that UTF-8 content, including Devanagari member names, is correctly recognized by spreadsheet applications such as Excel.

Actual Result

The CSV file starts directly with the CSV header rather than the required BOM.

Example:

72 65 66 ...

where:

72 = r
65 = e
66 = f

Therefore, the required UTF-8 BOM:

EF BB BF

is missing.

Impact

CSV consumers may incorrectly detect the file encoding. This can result in Unicode characters, such as Devanagari member names, being displayed incorrectly when the file is opened in spreadsheet applications.

Evidence

The exported CSV was inspected at the byte level.

Observed first bytes:

72 65 66

Expected first bytes:

EF BB BF
Specification Reference

app/SPEC.md §6.5 — CSV Export

The specification requires the CSV export to be UTF-8 with a byte-order mark.

Suggested Fix

Generate the CSV response with the UTF-8 BOM before the CSV header:

EF BB BF

The fix should also be regression-tested to ensure future CSV exports continue to contain the required BOM.

Risk

Medium — The booking data itself is not lost, but Unicode data may not be interpreted correctly by some CSV consumers, affecting data usability and interoperability.